.TH MW-REMEMBER 1 "2026-06-22" "MemoryWhale" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mw-remember \- record a single command run into local MemoryWhale storage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mw-remember
.RI [ --cwd
.IR path ]
.RI [ --exit-code
.IR code ]
.RI [ --stdout
.IR text ]
.RI [ --stderr
.IR text ]
.RI [ --notes
.IR text ]
.B --
.I command
.RI [ args... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mw-remember
stores one command run \(em its command and arguments, working directory,
exit code, captured output, and an optional note \(em into the local
MemoryWhale SQLite database. It is the low-level building block used by the
per-command shell hook and can also be called directly from scripts. Nothing
is uploaded.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--cwd " path
Working directory to record (default: the current directory).
.TP
.BI "--exit-code " code
.RB "Exit status of the command. Also accepts " --exit .
.TP
.BI "--stdout " text
Captured standard output to store.
.TP
.BI "--stderr " text
Captured standard error to store.
.TP
.BI "--notes " text
Free-form note attached to the run.
.TP
.BI "--from-hook " "claude|rho"
Read that client's hook JSON object from standard input and record a
matching bash/powershell tool call. Unknown or empty payloads are
ignored. Always exits 0 so a recording failure cannot block the agent.
.TP
.B --
Everything after this is the command and its arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mw-remember --cwd "$PWD" --exit-code 0 \\
  --notes "started the dev server" -- npm run dev -- --host 0.0.0.0
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mw (1),
.BR mw-serve (1)
